{
  "coverage": "covered",
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "Kinematic equations for constant acceleration relate an object's position, velocity, and acceleration when the acceleration does not change over time.",
      "sources": [
        "S7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Newton stated his second law in terms of momentum, indicating that the instantaneous rate at which a body's momentum changes is equal to the net force acting on the body.",
      "sources": [
        "S2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "This relationship is given by the vector equation $\\vec{F}_{net} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt}$.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2",
        "S3",
        "S6"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "missing": "",
  "outside": "",
  "check_question": "What is the vector equation for Newton's second law in terms of momentum?",
  "check_answer": "$\\vec{F}_{net} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt}$",
  "check_concept": "newtons-second-law"
}

{
  "coverage": "covered",
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "Newton's second law can be written in terms of momentum as the net force being equal to the time derivative of the momentum, given by $\\vec{F}_{net} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt}$.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2",
        "S3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "For a point mass, momentum is defined as the product of the mass and the velocity, $\\vec{p} = m\\vec{v}$.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2",
        "S3"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "missing": "",
  "outside": "",
  "check_question": "How is Newton's second law expressed using momentum?",
  "check_answer": "$\\vec{F}_{net} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt}$",
  "check_concept": "newtons-second-law"
}

{
  "coverage": "covered",
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "Newton stated his second law in terms of momentum as the instantaneous rate at which a body's momentum changes is equal to the net force acting on the body, given by the vector equation $\\vec{F}_{net} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt}$.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2",
        "S3"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "missing": "",
  "outside": "",
  "check_question": "If the net external force acting on a system is zero, what does Newton's second law in terms of momentum say about the time derivative of momentum $\\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt}$?",
  "check_answer": "It is equal to zero, meaning the momentum is constant.",
  "check_concept": "newtons-second-law"
}

{
  "coverage": "covered",
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "स्थैतिक घर्षण का अधिकतम मान $f_s(\\text{max}) = \\mu_s N$ होता है, जहाँ $\\mu_s$ स्थैतिक घर्षण गुणांक है और $N$ अभिलंब बल का परिमाण है।",
      "sources": [
        "S1"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "missing": "",
  "outside": "",
  "check_question": "यदि अभिलंब बल $N = 50\\text{ N}$ और स्थैतिक घर्षण गुणांक $\\mu_s = 0.4$ हो, तो स्थैतिक घर्षण का अधिकतम मान क्या होगा?",
  "check_answer": "20 N",
  "check_concept": "friction-forces"
}

{
  "coverage": "covered",
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "In free-fall motion, where air resistance and friction are negligible, all objects fall with the same constant acceleration toward the center of Earth, independent of their mass.",
      "sources": [
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Although people once believed that heavier objects have greater acceleration, heavy objects and lighter objects actually arrive at the ground at the same time when dropped from the same height.",
      "sources": [
        "S1"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "missing": "",
  "outside": "",
  "check_question": "If you drop a heavy rock and a light plastic ball in a vacuum where there is no air resistance, which one hits the ground first?",
  "check_answer": "They hit the ground at the same time.",
  "check_concept": "free-fall-motion"
}

{
  "coverage": "covered",
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "Friction ek aisi force hai jo contact surfaces ke beech relative motion ya attempted motion ko oppose karti hai. Jab do systems contact mein hain aur stationary hain relative to one another, toh unke beech ki friction ko static friction kehte hain.",
      "sources": [
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Jab do systems contact mein hain aur moving relative to one another hain, toh unke beech ki friction ko kinetic friction kehte hain. For example, friction slows a hockey puck sliding on ice.",
      "sources": [
        "S1"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "missing": "",
  "outside": "",
  "check_question": "Agar ek box floor par stationary hai aur aap us par force laga rahe hain, toh uss waqt kaunsi friction act kar rahi hogi?",
  "check_answer": "Static friction",
  "check_concept": "friction-forces"
}

{
  "coverage": "partial",
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "Newton's second law of motion says that the net external force on an object with a certain mass is directly proportional to and in the same direction as the acceleration of the object.",
      "sources": [
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "For the special case of a constant point-like mass, Newton's second law is written as $\\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$.",
      "sources": [
        "S2",
        "S3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Newton also stated his second law as the instantaneous rate at which a body's momentum changes being equal to the net force acting on the body, given by $\\vec{F}_{net} = \\frac{d\\vec{p}}{dt}$.",
      "sources": [
        "S5"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "missing": "The provided course material does not state the units for Newton's second law.",
  "outside": "",
  "check_question": "If a constant mass of $2\\text{ kg}$ experiences a net external force of $6\\text{ N}$, what is the magnitude of its acceleration?",
  "check_answer": "$3\\text{ m/s}^2$",
  "check_concept": "newtons-second-law"
}

{
  "coverage": "covered",
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "The acceleration can be found graphically from the slope of a velocity-versus-time graph.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2",
        "S5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "When the velocity-versus-time graph is a straight line, the slope is constant, which means the acceleration is also constant.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S2",
        "S5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "For a curve on a velocity-versus-time graph, instantaneous acceleration at any point is found by taking the slope of the tangent line at that point.",
      "sources": [
        "S3"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "missing": "",
  "outside": "",
  "check_question": "If a velocity-versus-time graph is a straight line starting at v = 0 m/s at t = 0 s and reaching v = 10 m/s at t = 2 s, what is the acceleration?",
  "check_answer": "5 m/s^2",
  "check_concept": "acceleration"
}

{
  "coverage": "covered",
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "The force of friction $f$ between the block and the rough surface opposes the direction of the applied force $F$, and the magnitude of the static friction balances that of the applied force.",
      "sources": [
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Diagram (a) shows impending motion with static friction, and diagram (b) shows the object moving to the right.",
      "sources": [
        "S1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Diagram (c) is a graph of frictional magnitude versus applied force $F$, showing a linear increase in the static region up to a peak labeled $f_{s,\text{max}} = \nu_s N$, followed by a drop in the kinetic region where $f_k = \nu_k N$.",
      "sources": [
        "S1"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "missing": "",
  "outside": "",
  "check_question": "If the applied force on a stationary block increases, what happens to the magnitude of the static friction force before it starts to slip?",
  "check_answer": "It increases to balance the applied force.",
  "check_concept": "friction-forces"
}

{
  "coverage": "covered",
  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "The work-energy theorem states that the net work done on a particle or system equals the change in its kinetic energy, expressed as $W_{\text{net}} = K_B - K_A$.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S5",
        "S6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "You can use this theorem to find properties of a system, such as final speed for any shaped frictionless surface, making calculations much simpler than trying to solve Newton's second law.",
      "sources": [
        "S1",
        "S3",
        "S4"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "missing": "",
  "outside": "",
  "check_question": "If an object speeds up as it moves, is the net work done on it positive, negative, or zero?",
  "check_answer": "Positive",
  "check_concept": "work-energy-theorem"
}
